{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And He remembered</b> — Rabbi Moses the Kohen said: the sense of <b>And He remembered that they were flesh</b> is that they have no strength to endure the wrath of YHWH, for they would die immediately, and this is the sense of <b>a passing spirit</b>. In my opinion: the body is the flesh, as in <b>and my glory also, my flesh</b>, and similarly <b>גושמית לא איתוהי</b> [it has no corporeality]." "<b>And He mentioned flesh</b> — because they desire the flesh, for that which is generated desires its likeness, that it may strengthen it. Furthermore: the measure of the spirit's binding with the flesh is not long-lasting; therefore they seek to take pleasure before they separate. And behold, the word <b>they</b> serves on account of another <b>they</b>: <b>they are a passing spirit</b>, for the human being is spirit and body, which is flesh, which is what feels."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 78, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:35:12.815791171Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}